eview could put lives at risk  the real problem is the delay in the  intelligence community review of the  damage  that this all caused the damage to our  national security and to our human  assets overseas but trump's attorney so  far unable to back up his claim that he  declassified the documents in question  that omission speaks loudly because his  lawyers obviously know that the  president doesn't have the ability to  just completely declassify things  meanwhile unlike his legal team outside  the courthouse trump has been vocal on  his social media platform explicitly  supporting q anon whose followers  believe satanic cannibal pedophiles  secretly run the government and showing  pictures of top democrats saying they're  the enemies and demanding he be  reinstated as president for donald trump  any coverage is good coverage now the  investigation for serious crimes that  could see the former president  incarcerated are not just being  downplayed in public but also before a  judge nitu garcia global news washington  for the first time astronomers captured  an image of a planet outside our solar  system using the new web space telescope  the images well they're not much to look  at but what they represent is amazing  that fuzzy dot is an infrared image of a  gas giant type planet orbiting a star  about 400 light years away from earth as  the web telescope continues to get  dialed in we should get images of much  smaller planets and an even greater  detail including those that could harbor  life  fighting for fair learning ahead is  canada's education system failing its  students with disabilities  every parent wants the best for their  child but a newfoundland and labrador  couple are fighting for fair education  todd and kimberly churchill have long  been calling for their son carter who is  deaf to receive proper assistance in the  classroom so he can get the same quality  education as children with hearing as  ross lord reports the province's human  rights commission has begun looking into  the discrimination case launched against  the province's english school district  aside from living with cerebral palsy  carter churchill is deaf the condition  his parents say they were assured would  not diminish his education but they say  it did they allege the eastern school  district in the saint john's area of  newfoundland and labrador violated the  human rights act by providing  substandard education to carter he is  going to grade six next week and he's  not able to read he is not meeting the  curriculum in his grade level at their  human rights hearing testimony confirmed  carter received a student assistant in  kindergarten to translate for him in  sign language as it turned out the  assistant could not so much as spell out  her own name leaving carter isolated and  falling behind on his learning the  church he'll say in the past five years  such problems have continued they say  all they want for their son is what was  promised in 2010 canada ratified a  united nations convention promising  people with disabilities an inclusive  quality education on an equal basis with  others when carter is older  and we explain all this to him of all  the fighting we had to do for his  education  we need to be able to look him in his  eyes  and tell him we did the best we could  with many so-called schools for the deaf  closing across canada in the name of  inclusion and the pandemic compounding  educational challenges advocates say  shortcomings are arguably more acute  than ever i think a lot of these  students are  going through the education system and  just being ignored  so i think there are probably a lot more  stories like the churchill's that  we're not even aware of the churchill's  hope the human rights commission's  ruling calls out inadequate programming  spurring action that spares other  families from the financial and  emotional strain of fighting the system  for a fair education  ross lord club news  50 years after tragedy next montreal  remembers those lost to the bluebird  cafe fire  in montreal today a tragic anniversary  for one of the worst mass killings in  canadian history on september 1st 1972  after being turned away at the door  three men intentionally set fire to a  packed downtown bar  50 years later our mike armstrong spoke  with witnesses and survivors remembering  the victims of the blue bird cafe fire  i was finishing my day at work it is a  night this retired police officer has  never been able to shake 50 years ago  andre mayville turned a corner and saw  he says a ball of fire and people in the  flames people were falling from the  second floor they jumped from the window  i was not able to help them too much  the bluebird building stood on this spot  in downtown montreal on the second floor  there was a nightclub the wagon wheel  september 1st 1972 was the friday night  of a long weekend there was a band  playing and suddenly the drummer yelled  fire  to me it's like a stampede you're  literally  running for your life brent malfara was  inside the club on that night trying to  escape what he says felt like an oven  his face was burned i had third degree  it was just from the heat and i remember  the fire the guys coming saying you  should you want to go to the hospital i  go no no  you got more important people to take  care of right now  there was just everybody in a panic the  tragedy was remembered with a ceremony  just up the street from where the bar  once stood there is today a monument to  the 37 lives lost in a crime as sad as  it was senseless  the fire was set by three men who had  earlier in the night being turned away  at the door they were too drunk hoping  to put a scare into the doorman they  returned with a plastic jug and 50 cents  worth of gas they set the staircase on  fire two emergency exits in the building  had been blocked to keep people out that  also kept people in  the drummer who yelled fire was killed  the band's guitar player don bedard made  it out after an exit door was forced  open somebody got on top of somebody's  shoulder and broke the door and  thank god they did that because i'd be  dead  the three arsonists would be sentenced  to life in prison but all were released  on parole within 10 years the loss of  life led to changes in fire regulations  but for decades it was known as  montreal's forgotten tragedy  but did not feel forgotten on this day  like armstrong global news montreal  and that's global national for this  thursday night i'm faran osser on behalf  of our whole crew i want to thank you so  much for spending part of your evening  with us  tonight's your canada well look at this  photo it's harvest in golden prairie  saskatchewan until tomorrow take care of  yourselves and each other  good night  [Music]  you
 

